[core]
default_agent = "CodeActAgent"
max_iterations = 1000
max_budget_per_task = 10000
workspace_base = "/linux"
runtime = "local"
enable_browser = false

[agent]
# CodeActAgent configuration
enable_prompt_extensions = true
enable_mcp = false
enable_browsing = false

[kArena]
instance_template = """
The Linux repository is checked out at `/linux`. Syzbot finds a reproducer that triggers a crash:

{{problem_statement}}

Can you help me investigate the kernel crash? Your task is to make the minimal changes to the repository to fix the crash.

Take the following debugging pattern to guide your task:
1. Take a look at the reproducer and crash report. Consider a kernel crash as a result of the reproducer setting up an environment, and the kernel gives the crash report when it runs into a failing assertion/sanitizer failure. Therefore, reading kernel code with the goal of figuring out each system call (especially the last system call) in the reproducer is essential. You need to read functions relevant to those system calls and understand what kernel states were constructed for the later kernel panic. You can search the corresponding system call handler in the repository first, and then read the code from the handler to intermediate helper functions, and finally to the crashing function.
2. When you believe you have a picture of the whole crashing control path, reason the problem of the control path. Common cases include the following:
    a. The control path should not have been executed --- because of corrupt kernel states set up earlier by the reproducer. The corrupt states can be anything: wrong values were assigned, or right values should have been assigned.
    b. A certain function in the control path may not have sanitized invalid requests.
    c. ...
3. Come up with a patch that fixes the problem. Please, check if your proposed patch affects normal functionalities. You should come up with a solution that remedies the buggy behavior only. For kernel crashes caused by `WARN`, `WARN_ON_ONCE`, `BUG_ON`, etc., YOU SHOULD NOT REMOVE ASSERTIONS THAT CAUSED THE KERNEL PANIC, or you will be CHEATING. The assertions are oracles from developers to do sanity checking for the kernel internal states. Reject any solution that will lead to kernel malfunction! You can revise your patch as needed.

When you finish writing a patch, use bash tool to run script `/KBDr/run_kernel` (no arguments needed) with timeout of 45 minutes (2700 seconds) to validate the current patch. kGym will run the modified kernel and give you feedback.
It is important NOT TO run your own validation. You're running inside a sandbox and the sandbox does not run on the buggy kernel commit. You will not get any feedback from running any reproducer locally.
Therefore, run `run_kernel` script (no arguments needed) to get feedback.

If `/KBDr/run_kernel` reports no crash reproduced, review your patch to see if the patch affects normal functionalities. If not, you may submit the patch.
If not, you can keep going on revising your patch.
"""
